043 #edb/ref 17592186125697 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124718 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124718 #edb/ref 1045 "Inhibited Growth Models With First-Order ODEs" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124718 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186125687 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124718 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049406 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124718 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "05a41e7f-1bb0-564c-965f-28e61f0c9c67" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124762 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186121169 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124762 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106661" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124762 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $y < 150$ and $t \\ge 0$, which of the following is an inhibited growth equation?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124762 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3ff73a32-c10e-5c6a-a056-14d519235f78" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124763 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186121175 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124763 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186397850 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124763 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106662" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124763 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124763 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $y<80$ and $t\\ge 0,$ which of the following is an inhibited growth equation?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124763 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "092a8c01-7fc1-532c-ba20-9cb4c823a717" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124763 #edb/ref 1074 "An inhibited growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=k(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ for all $t\\ge 0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited growth equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=400-5y.\n$$\n\nTo see this, let's factor the right-hand side of the equation. This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=5(80-y).\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=5,$ and $a=80.$ Note that for inhibited growth, we must have $y<80$ for all $t\\ge 0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124766 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186121193 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124766 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106691" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124766 #edb/ref 1071 "A factory's management noted that the growth of the unit production is directly proportional to the difference between the maximum number of units a factory worker can produce and the current production quantity. Let $P(t)$ be the production quantity, measured in units produced, and let $t \\ge 0$ be the time in days. If the maximum number of units that a worker can produce is $70$ $units/day$, which differential equation could be used to model the factory's production? Assume that $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124766 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "19a4e47b-fcd5-50aa-804a-b275eeea23d5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125068 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186123008 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125068 #edb/ref 1069 "q-37389" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125068 #edb/ref 1071 "A poultry farmer has egg-laying chickens. She knows that her farm can produce a maximum of $500$ eggs a day; however, it is far behind it.\nSince she started monitoring, the chickens laid $390$ eggs and $400$ eggs on days $3$ and $6$, respectively. How many eggs do the chickens lay on day $42$?\nRound your answer to the nearest integer.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the egg production $E(t)$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t$ is the time, in days.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125068 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3ce256b3-4ef6-51c4-ab72-48603b95ab11" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125069 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323559 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125069 #edb/ref 1069 "q-37400" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125069 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125069 #edb/ref 1071 "The population of students $P(t)$ in a school can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.016(2\\,500-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, measured in years. If the initial population of the school is $800$ students, calculate the population after $4$ years. Round your answer to the nearest integer." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125069 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7d1a929a-56b1-5075-bd38-17c32dd77741" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125069 #edb/ref 1074 "We're told that the population in the school is modeled by the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.016(2\\,500-P).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{2\\,500-P} & =0.016\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dP}{2\\,500-P} & =\\int 0.016\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2\\,500-P| & =0.016t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $2\\,500-P>0$ for all $t\\ge 0.$\nNext, we solve for $P(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2500-P & ={e}^{-0.016t-C} \\\\ 2500-P & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-0.016t} \\\\ 2,500-P & =K{e}^{-0.016t} \\\\ P & =2\\,500-K{e}^{-0.016t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{-C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the initial population of the school was $800$ students. So, we apply the initial condition $P(0)=800,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}800 & =2\\,500-K{e}^{0} \\\\ 800 & =2\\,500-K \\\\ K & =1\\,700\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nP(t)=2\\,500-1\\,700{e}^{-0.016t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the population in the school after $t=4$ years is\n\n$$\nP(4)=2\\,500-1\\,700{e}^{-0.016\\cdot 4}≈905\\,\\text{students},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125687 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125688 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125687 #edb/ref 1061 602732 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125687 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "dece1059-cefb-5a42-bee0-3e8ae69a26de" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125687 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125811 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125688 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125689 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125688 #edb/ref 1061 602733 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125688 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4c3e192b-1705-5544-8a07-e6dda0b52232" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125688 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124673 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125689 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125697 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125689 #edb/ref 1061 602737 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125689 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7b6c2c69-a8ff-52d4-bd1f-03307b01cf1d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125689 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125810 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125690 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125691 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125690 #edb/ref 1061 602738 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125690 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "35100ce2-3688-5414-82d2-5da53db91c64" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125690 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124674 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125691 #edb/ref 1061 602741 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125691 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7489936a-e434-5105-9224-089b6c6f0306" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125691 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124675 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125697 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125690 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125697 #edb/ref 1061 608081 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125697 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c14bd17a-37a8-5dd9-8282-3be55f3d8fd6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125697 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125711 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125711 #edb/ref 1064 "Our general solution for the wolf population in month $t$ is $P(t) = 450 - Ke^{-kt}.$ Now, let's find the specific values of our constants.\n\nWe're told that, in month $6,$ the population was $392.$ So, applying the condition $P(6) = 392,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(6) & =392 \\\\ 450-K{e}^{-6k} & =392 \\\\ K{e}^{-6k} & =58.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, in month $12,$ the population was $420.$ So, applying the condition $P(12) = 420,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(12) & =420 \\\\ 450-K{e}^{-12k} & =420 \\\\ K{e}^{-12k} & =30.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, by dividing the result of the first condition by the result of the second, we can solve for $k{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{K{e}^{-6k}}{K{e}^{-12k}} & =\\frac{58}{30} \\\\ {e}^{6k} & =\\frac{29}{15} \\\\ 6k & =\\operatorname{ln}(\\frac{29}{15}) \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{ln}(\\frac{29}{15}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting back into the result of the first condition, we solve for $K{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}K & =58{e}^{6k} \\\\  & =58(\\frac{29}{15}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1682}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the wolf population in month $t$ is\n\n$$\nP(t) = 450 - \\frac{1682}{15}e^{-(t/6)\\ln(29/15)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, let [math]m[/math] be the month when the population reaches [math]435[/math] wolves. Then, we have\n\n\\begin{align*}\n435 &= 450 - \\frac{1682}{15}e^{-km} \\\\[5pt]\n15 &= \\frac{1682}{15}e^{-km} \\\\[5pt]\ne^{-km} &= \\frac{225}{1682} \\\\[5pt]\n-km &= \\ln \\left(\\frac{225}{1682}\\right) \\\\[5pt]\nm &= -\\frac{1}{k}\\ln \\left(\\frac{225}{1682}\\right).\n\\end{align*}\n\nSubstituting in our value for [math]k,[/math] we conclude that the population will be [math]435[/math] wolves after approximately\n\\[\nm = \\dfrac{-\\ln\\left(\\dfrac{225}{1682}\\right)}{\\dfrac{1}{6}\\ln\\left(\\dfrac{29}{15}\\right)}\n=18.3\\ldots\n\\approx 18\n\\]\nmonths, rounded to the nearest month." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125711 #edb/ref 1065 14905 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125711 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "4cf455fa-240c-5cab-994e-c0447e7d8788" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125711 #edb/ref 1067 "Modeling Inhibited Growth (Continued)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125810 #edb/ref 1064 "Now that we’ve practiced identifying inhibited growth models, let’s work through an example to see how they are used in practice.\n\nSuppose a wildlife reserve is tracking the population of a reintroduced wolf pack. The environment can support a maximum population of $450$ wolves, but the current population is still below this limit. Since monitoring began, the population reached $392$ wolves after $6$ months and $420$ wolves after $12$ months.\n\nLet's construct an inhibited growth model to describe the wolf population in terms of the month $t.$\n\nThe rate at which the population $P(t)$ changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum population and its current population. Therefore, we have the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}P}{\\mathrm{d}t}=k(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum population the reserve can sustain, and $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=450,$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}P}{\\mathrm{d}t}=k(450-P).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{450-P} & =k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dP}{450-P} & =\\int k\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}|450-P| & =kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $450 - P > 0$ for all $t \\geq 0.$ Next, we solve for $P(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}450-P & ={e}^{-kt-C} \\\\ 450-P & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ 450-P & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ P & =450-K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K=e^{-C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nThis is the general solution. On the next slide, we will use the population data to find the specific values for the constants $K$ and $k.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125810 #edb/ref 1065 4076 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125810 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "91be4d2b-a528-55eb-a5f4-1e02178fad56" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125810 #edb/ref 1067 "Modeling Inhibited Growth" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125811 #edb/ref 1064 "In many real situations, a quantity increases quickly at first, but then the increase slows down and the quantity levels off near some fixed value.\n\nFor example, suppose a cold drink is taken out of a refrigerator and placed in a warm room. At first, its temperature rises rapidly because the difference between the drink's temperature and the room temperature is large. As the drink warms up, this difference decreases, and the warming slows down. Eventually, the temperature levels off and approaches the room temperature.\n\nThis type of behavior is described by an **inhibited growth model**.\n\nAn *inhibited growth equation* models situations where the rate at which a quantity $P$ increases depends on how far it is from a target value $a$, called the **equilibrium value**. This equilibrium value represents the level that the quantity approaches over time.\n\nAn inhibited growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}P}{\\textrm{d}t} = r(a-P),\n$$\n\nand an inhibited growth initial value problem has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}P}{\\textrm{d}t} = r(a-P), \\qquad P(0) = P_0\n$$\n\nwhere $r > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $P < a$ for all $t > 0.$\n\nIn this model, the equilibrium value $a$ acts as an upper bound. The solution increases over time but never rises above $a$, instead approaching it more and more slowly. The figure below illustrates this behavior.\n\n![](images/81/815441d6991917ade1594f1c60a90e9505577917b1088cae3b10c637b4486c15.png)\n\n**Note:** Inhibited growth and inhibited decay use the same basic idea: the rate of change is proportional to the difference between $P$ and the equilibrium value $a$. The only difference is whether the solution approaches $a$ from below (growth) or from above (decay).\n\nInhibited growth models can be written in expanded form. To identify an expanded equation as inhibited growth, we factorise out the *positive* coefficient of $P.$ For example, the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}P}{\\textrm{d}t} = 60-12P,\n$$\n\n*is* an inhibited growth equation for $P < 5$ and $t \\geq 0,$ since it can be expressed with $r=12$ and $a=5$ as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}P}{\\textrm{d}t} = 12(5-P).\n$$\n\nNow let's work through a few examples to get some practice identifying inhibited growth models." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125811 #edb/ref 1065 4077 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125811 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "9afedae3-a674-5a44-9d7a-4cdf57e66285" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125811 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305194 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{dT}{dt}=k(95-T)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305194 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305194 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "04aedd39-6cd0-5b6c-a95b-8f1528463bef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305298 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{dT}{dt}=k(30-T)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305298 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305298 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "100e6d8a-679a-59a7-9f49-904c4ba960dd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305744 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{dT}{dt}=k(60-T)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305744 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305744 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3d8045ca-7194-5335-8e8c-fb093e38eb2b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186306336 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{dT}{dt}=k(100-T)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186306336 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186306336 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7c223239-ad05-5364-8640-ceef9053e8b9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186307318 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{dT}{dt}=k(950-T)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186307318 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186307318 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "eb832421-670f-5c24-a2cd-1bebc2d73fe8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186307862 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186305194 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186307862 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186305298 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186307862 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186305744 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186307862 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186306336 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186307862 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186307318 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186307862 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186307862 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186307862 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186305194 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186307862 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "69e66b97-0745-5e34-a590-bf57a3e5e855" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308739 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307862 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308739 #edb/ref 1069 "q-335142" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308739 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308739 #edb/ref 1071 "A tutoring center currently has $10$ enrolled students. The number of students in the center $T(t)$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in months. If the maximum capacity of the tutoring center is $95$ students, which differential equation could be used to model the student population of the center? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308739 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5651275c-fd64-5799-85e0-52ab15ebd288" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308739 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the number of students in the center obeys an inhibited growth model, the rate at which the number of students changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum capacity and the number of students in the center. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=k(a-T),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum capacity of the tutoring center, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=95$ students, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=k(95-T).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315254 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t}=-k(100-F)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315254 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315254 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "17d8e18d-55da-5747-a381-b32f0cd64912" #edb/ref 175921