472 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317472 #edb/ref 1071 "An ecologist found that the growth of the number of frogs in a wetland is directly proportional to the difference between the wetland’s maximum frog population and the current population. Let $F(t)$ be the frog population, measured in number of frogs, and let $t\\ge 0$ be the time in years. If the maximum frog population the wetland can support is $100$ frogs, which differential equation could be used to model the frog population? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317472 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a2bd5b32-0a6c-5631-90f3-4c8beb909e62" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317472 #edb/ref 1074 "The rate at which the frog population $F(t)$ of the wetland changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum frog population the wetland can support and the current frog population. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t}=k(a-F),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum frog population the wetland can support, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=100\\text{ frogs},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t}=k(100-F).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321161 #edb/ref 1080 "$986$ students" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321161 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321161 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0160e30a-5846-5aa6-86ef-a585cedd44ba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322037 #edb/ref 1080 "$905$ students" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322037 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322037 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "71929081-a7d5-5326-a8f5-4740571cce74" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322096 #edb/ref 1080 "$1005$ students" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322096 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322096 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "78c60709-1e3a-58f3-8b53-ecf0dc4c70c