"\\frac{dy}{dt}=5-400y" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397851 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397851 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "fd4d9520-8eb8-590a-8484-786df4886f48" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397852 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{dy}{dt}=5y-400" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397852 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397852 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4b2d8367-f11c-56cb-b66f-27a8c632bc41" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397853 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{dy}{dt}=400-5y" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397853 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397853 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "906da5cb-d9e8-5ec3-b4fd-cd8abe7cdb40" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397854 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{dy}{dt}=5{y}^{2}-400" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397854 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397854 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "fa1db174-5614-5103-8d7e-cb38f5042fa5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397855 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{dy}{dt}=400-5{y}^{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397855 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397855 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a5c36cb8-43dc-59ec-b220-e1d3bce29295" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402133 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186402134 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402133 #edb/ref 1069 "q-335542" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402133 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402133 #edb/ref 1071 "A wildlife reserve can support a total of $800$ deer.\nAt the beginning of a disease study in the reserve, a parasitic infection has affected $80$ deer. After $10$ days, the number of deer affected by the parasite increases to $300.$ To the nearest day, how long does it take for the infected deer population to reach $600$ animals?\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the number of deer affected by the parasite, $P(t),$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time, in days.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402133 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a4fd0915-73cc-575a-b3cc-4a156a96dd49" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402133 #edb/ref 1074 "The rate at which the deer population affected by the parasite, $P(t),$ changes is proportional to the difference between the total deer population and its current population affected. Therefore, we have the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the total deer population in the reserve, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=800$ deer, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(800-P).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{800-P} & =k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dP}{800-P} & =\\int k\\,dt \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|800-P| & =kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $800-P>0$ for all $t\\ge 0.$\nNext, we solve for $P(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}800-P & ={e}^{-kt-C} \\\\ 800-P & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ 800-P & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ P & =800-K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{-C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial deer population affected by the parasite was $80$ animals. So, we apply the initial condition $P(0)=80,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =80 \\\\ 800-K{e}^{0} & =80 \\\\ 800-K & =80 \\\\ K & =720\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $P(t):$\n\n$$\nP(t)=800-720{e}^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $10$ days, the deer population affected by the parasite was $300$ animals. So, applying the condition $P(10)=300,$ we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(10) & =300 \\\\ 800-720{e}^{-10k} & =300 \\\\ 500 & =720{e}^{-10k} \\\\ {e}^{-10k} & =\\frac{500}{720}=\\frac{25}{36} \\\\ -10k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{25}{36}) \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{25}{36})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when $600$ deer are affected by the parasite. Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({t}_{1}) & =600 \\\\ 800-720{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =600 \\\\ 200 & =720{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} \\\\ {e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{200}{720}=\\frac{5}{18} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{18}) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{18}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that $600$ deer will be affected by the parasite after\n\n$$\n{t}_{1}=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{18})}{-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{25}{36})}=35.128…≈35\\,\\text{days},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest day." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402134 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186402135 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402134 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186402136 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402134 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186402137 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402134 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186402138 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402134 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186402139 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402134 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402134 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402134 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186402135 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402134 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "63531a29-5d3e-5f5f-97ce-ef01b7d03202" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402135 #edb/ref 1080 "$35$ days" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402135 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402135 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "088dc229-13bb-525f-97f0-615a3387e9fc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402136 #edb/ref 1080 "$50$ days" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402136 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402136 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "dbe81525-8780-55b2-9e79-394a40fd9a7e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402137 #edb/ref 1080 "$40$ days" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1759218640